{
  "dom_id": "step-q171701",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14167856/assets/8d59cf7d27ed6e7912a53e6ef52a2193f372c15fca3d50272d3c697451ff2db5.png)\n\nGiven that the slope of the line shown above is $-3,$ the equation of the line is $y=${{field-1}}\n$x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ We know that the slope is $m=-3,$ so we just need to work out $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-2).$ So, the $y$-intercept is $b=-2.$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-3$ and the $y$-intercept $b=-2$ into the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\ny=-3x-2\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
